    {
      "id": "rOZoua6QjqAO5sa26RD5f",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1260.6444465306802,
      "y": 153.5260968512498,
      "width": 503.47967529296875,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2a",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1952772937,
      "version": 305,
      "versionNonce": 1173006381,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787438555794,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "API\n\nPOST /upload {video, videoMeatdata}\nGET /videos/{videoId} --> {video/videoMetadata}",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "API\n\nPOST /upload {video, videoMeatdata}\nGET /videos/{videoId} --> {video/videoMetadata}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vCfvdqtJ2ojkqO4b4Uep_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1084.1614803651898,
      "y": 898.1715949507281,
      "width": 1505.9022563891656,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2125685577,
      "version": 530,
      "versionNonce": 1782685667,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787439261164,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n- post-processes videos by splitting them into small segments (each a playable unit that's a few seconds in length) and then converts each segment into\ndifferent formats playable on different devices\n\n\n\n- Adaptive bitrate streaming\n- The client will fetch the VideoMetadata, which will have a URL pointing to the manifest file in S3.\n- The client will download the manifest file.\n- The client will choose a format based on network conditions / user settings. \n- The client retrieves the URL for this segment in its chosen format from the manifest file. The client will download the first segment.\n- The client will play that segment and begin downloading more segments.\nIf the client detects that network conditions are slowing down (or improving), it will vary the format of the video segments it is downloading. \nIf network conditions get worse (e.g. the bitrate is lower), the client will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any\ninterruption in streaming.\n\n\n\n1/ The client would divide the video file into chunks, each with a fingerprint hash. A chunk would be small, ~5-10MB in size.\n2/ VideoMetadata would have a field called chunks which would be a list of chunk JSONs, each with fingerprint and status field.\n3/ The client would POST request to the backend to update the VideoMetadata with the list of chunks, each with status NotUploaded.\n4/ The client would upload each chunk to S3.\n5/ When S3 acknowledges a part upload it returns the part number and an ETag. \nThe client relays that response to the backend (e.g. PATCH /videos/{id}/chunks) so the server can verify the fingerprint/ETag \nvia S3 APIs and then update the matching chunk record to Uploaded.\n6/ Once the client calls CompleteMultipartUpload, S3 emits an object-level notification (e.g. ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload) exactly once per\nobject. \nThat single event can kick off downstream processing, but chunk-level progress tracking remains client-driven as described above.\n7/ If the client stops uploading, it can resume by fetching the VideoMetadata to see the uploaded chunks and to skip chunks that had been uploaded\nalready.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n- post-processes videos by splitting them into small segments (each a playable unit that's a few seconds in length) and then converts each segment into different formats playable on different devices\n\n\n\n- Adaptive bitrate streaming\n- The client will fetch the VideoMetadata, which will have a URL pointing to the manifest file in S3.\n- The client will download the manifest file.\n- The client will choose a format based on network conditions / user settings. \n- The client retrieves the URL for this segment in its chosen format from the manifest file. The client will download the first segment.\n- The client will play that segment and begin downloading more segments.\nIf the client detects that network conditions are slowing down (or improving), it will vary the format of the video segments it is downloading. \nIf network conditions get worse (e.g. the bitrate is lower), the client will attempt to download more compressed, lower resolution segments to avoid any interruption in streaming.\n\n\n\n1/ The client would divide the video file into chunks, each with a fingerprint hash. A chunk would be small, ~5-10MB in size.\n2/ VideoMetadata would have a field called chunks which would be a list of chunk JSONs, each with fingerprint and status field.\n3/ The client would POST request to the backend to update the VideoMetadata with the list of chunks, each with status NotUploaded.\n4/ The client would upload each chunk to S3.\n5/ When S3 acknowledges a part upload it returns the part number and an ETag. \nThe client relays that response to the backend (e.g. PATCH /videos/{id}/chunks) so the server can verify the fingerprint/ETag \nvia S3 APIs and then update the matching chunk record to Uploaded.\n6/ Once the client calls CompleteMultipartUpload, S3 emits an object-level notification (e.g. ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload) exactly once per object. \nThat single event can kick off downstream processing, but chunk-level progress tracking remains client-driven as described above.\n7/ If the client stops uploading, it can resume by fetching the VideoMetadata to see the uploaded chunks and to skip chunks that had been uploaded already.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "29ys9YqSAjQwYp79nPfuh",
